# RuleBeat: environment variables
# Copy to packages/web/.env.local for local development:  cp .env.example packages/web/.env.local
#
# Every variable here is optional for a Docker install with its data volume: start the container,
# read the generated admin password from data/initial-password.txt (the log names the file, never
# the password), sign in, and configure the rest from Settings. Env always wins over the console.
#
# Set variables for a deployment that must arrive already configured, or for one with no volume
# (Azure Container Apps, App Service, Container Instances), where AUTH_SECRET,
# RULEBEAT_ENCRYPTION_KEY and RULEBEAT_INITIAL_PASSWORD are required alongside the database URL.
#
# Every variable is explained in docs/public/configure.md, which opens with a reference table of
# all of them and a "required when" column. Anchors below point into that file.

# ── Database (configure.md#database-backend) ──────────────────────────────────
# Unset: SQLite in the data volume. A postgres:// URL: PostgreSQL. Switching later is a fresh install.
# RULEBEAT_DATABASE_URL=postgres://rulebeat:password@postgres:5432/rulebeat
# The same string from a mounted file, keeping the password out of the environment.
# RULEBEAT_DATABASE_URL_FILE=/run/secrets/rulebeat_database_url

# ── Session and encryption (configure.md#making-a-random-value) ───────────────
# Both are generated into the data volume when unset and required with no volume. Any string of
# 32 or more random characters is valid: openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Signs the session cookie. Changing it signs everyone out.
AUTH_SECRET=
# AUTH_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/auth_secret
#
# Encrypts credentials entered in the console. Losing it makes every stored credential unreadable.
# See configure.md#rotating-secrets before changing it on a running install.
# RULEBEAT_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# RULEBEAT_ENCRYPTION_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/rulebeat_encryption_key

# ── First admin (configure.md#local-sign-in-and-the-first-admin) ──────────────
# Password for the seeded admin@rulebeat.local instead of a generated one. Required with no volume.
# Still changed on first sign-in.
# RULEBEAT_INITIAL_PASSWORD=
# Work email that becomes an admin on first Microsoft sign-in; also the recovery path if every
# admin is removed.
# RULEBEAT_INITIAL_ADMIN=you@company.com
# Escape hatch when Microsoft sign-in breaks under a local sign-in policy of "disabled".
# RULEBEAT_FORCE_LOCAL_SIGNIN=true

# ── Public URL (configure.md#sign-in) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Used to build the Microsoft sign-in redirect. Set here or in Settings → Sign-in. There is no
# AUTH_TRUST_HOST: the forwarded host is always trusted, safely, because Entra pins the callback.
# AUTH_URL=https://rulebeat.example.com

# ── Azure scanning (configure.md#azure-scanning-credential) ───────────────────
# Reader role only. RuleBeat never creates the identity and never assigns itself a role. Pick ONE
# option, best first, or leave all unset and connect from Settings → Azure connection. On a
# developer machine `az login` is picked up automatically.
#
# The tenant to scan. With a managed identity this is the only variable needed.
AZURE_TENANT_ID=
#
# 1. Managed identity (best): enable it on the host, grant Reader, set nothing else.
#
# 2. Workload identity federation (keyless, for hosts outside Azure that issue OIDC tokens):
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_FEDERATED_TOKEN_FILE=/var/run/secrets/azure/tokens/azure-identity-token
#
# 3. Certificate:
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_PATH=/run/secrets/rulebeat.pem
# AZURE_CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD=
#
# 4. Client secret from a mounted file. Re-read on every use, so it rotates without a restart, and
#    invisible to `docker inspect`. See docker-compose.yml for the secrets block.
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/azure_client_secret
#
# 5. Client secret in plain text (last resort; on App Service the value can be a Key Vault
#    reference). Create the service principal with:
#      az ad sp create-for-rbac --name "RuleBeat" --role Reader --scopes /subscriptions/<subscription-id>
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# Directory rules additionally need the Microsoft Graph Application.Read.All permission on the same
# identity, with admin consent. See docs/public/permissions.md.

# ── Microsoft sign-in (configure.md#microsoft-entra-id-sign-in-optional) ──────
# All three together make Microsoft sign-in work from first boot. Or configure it from Settings →
# Sign-in, which also verifies the redirect URI: <AUTH_URL>/api/auth/callback/microsoft-entra-id
AUTH_MICROSOFT_ENTRA_ID_ID=
AUTH_MICROSOFT_ENTRA_ID_SECRET=
AUTH_MICROSOFT_ENTRA_ID_TENANT_ID=
# The secret from a mounted file instead. Re-read on every sign-in.
# AUTH_MICROSOFT_ENTRA_ID_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/entra_client_secret

# ── Behaviour ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Runs kept per category in Run History (configure.md#scan-history-retention). Default 90.
# SCAN_HISTORY_LIMIT=90
# Demo mode: anonymous, read-only, synthetic data, no real tenant (docs/public/demo-mode.md).
# RULEBEAT_DEMO=1
# Stops this replica's scheduler. Only for a second replica, which is not a supported topology.
# RULEBEAT_DISABLE_SCHEDULER=1
